            <ArticleTitle>Efficacy of fractional flow reserve measurements at side branch vessels treated with the crush stenting technique in true coronary bifurcation lesions.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>490-4</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1002/clc.20799</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Measurement of fractional flow reserve (FFR) has been frequently used to optimize the results of coronary stenting in patients with significant narrowing of coronary arteries.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="HYPOTHESIS" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">There has been a consensus that an FFR value &gt; 0.90 after stenting is a useful surrogate for favorable long-term clinical outcome. We evaluated the efficacy of FFR measurement at side branch vessels of true coronary bifurcation lesions that were treated with the crush stenting technique.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">This study included 12 patients with significant narrowing in both a main coronary vessel and side branch vessel who underwent the crush stenting procedure.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">After crush stenting, FFR measurement was performed at the side branch vessel prior to and after kissing balloon angioplasty (KBA). FFR values increased significantly, from 0.94 +/- 0.04 pre-KBA to 0.97 +/- 0.03 post-KBA (P = 0.011). FFR values after crush stenting but prior to KBA already measured &gt; 0.90 in 9 of the 12 patients (75%). FFR values for the remaining 3 patients were 0.88, 0.88, and 0.90, respectively.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">FFR measurement at side branch vessels of coronary bifurcation lesions treated with crush stenting may not contribute to adequate decision-making for improvement of long-term clinical outcomes. KBA should be strongly considered for patients with bifurcation lesions treated with crush stenting.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
